/E (Vertical third-man combinations: Once the opposition is stretched, Alonso's team looks to play quick combinations through the middle or between the lines. These are often short, sharp passes that exploit the spaces created by the baited press. Leverkusen's play often starts with the center-backs. As they progress the ball forward, they look for one of the players in the double pivot. Opposition teams might try to press these midfielders or block the direct passing lanes. This is where the third-man comes into play. With the first midfielder \(from the double pivot\) receiving the ball, he draws in opposition players. As they approach, he can lay the ball off to the second midfielder or another nearby player, creating a quick one-two passing sequence. As the second midfielder receives the ball, there's already a third player \(it could be an attacking midfielder, winger tucked inside, or even a forward dropping deep\) making a movement into space. The ball is then quickly relayed to this third-man. Once the player is in an advanced position, a through ball will be played in to the final third, with striker or other attacking players make a run in behind the defensive lines. )
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
